WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.133
Open model or frontier API?

2
00:00:02.413 --> 00:00:05.613
Teams choose open models for four structural reasons.

3
00:00:05.893 --> 00:00:19.696
Privacy and data control: prompts and outputs never leave infrastructure you control, which matters for regulated data, contracts that forbid third-party processing, and anything you would not paste into someone else’s service.

4
00:00:19.976 --> 00:00:26.824
Offline and edge use: a model on the device works without a network, on a plane, in a clinic, or in a factory.

5
00:00:27.104 --> 00:00:36.747
Customization: with the weights you can fine-tune on your data (weeks 2 and 3 of this studio), quantize for your hardware, or constrain decoding however you like.

6
00:00:37.027 --> 00:00:43.789
Version control: the weights you tested are the weights that run, for as long as you want, with no deprecation schedule.

7
00:00:44.069 --> 00:00:48.037
Cost at scale is the fifth reason, and the one most often misjudged.

8
00:00:48.317 --> 00:00:50.131
An API charges per token.

9
00:00:50.411 --> 00:00:55.061
Self-hosting charges for hardware and operations whether you use it or not.

10
00:00:55.341 --> 00:01:04.195
Self-hosting only wins when volume is high and steady enough to keep the hardware busy, and when the API you are comparing against is expensive.

11
00:01:04.475 --> 00:01:16.165
Chip Huyen’s AI Engineering frames this as a build-versus-buy decision that also counts engineering time, monitoring, and the safety work an API provider would otherwise do.

12
00:01:16.445 --> 00:01:19.667
The case for frontier APIs is equally concrete.

13
00:01:19.947 --> 00:01:32.320
The strongest frontier models still lead on the hardest reasoning, coding, and agentic tasks; someone else runs, scales, and secures the serving stack; and you get new capabilities without migrating.

14
00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:43.117
For most teams the answer is a mix: a frontier API where quality decides, and a small open model where privacy, offline use, latency, or volume decide.

15
00:01:43.397 --> 00:01:53.872
The way to choose is the one this program keeps returning to: build a small eval from real examples and measure quality, cost per task, and latency for each candidate.
